  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Smoke continues to impact the region. Northwest Montana gets a\n  short break from the smoke today.\n \n- Warming, drying trend will take place through the week.\n\n- Breezy conditions at the end of the week pose a fire weather\n  risk starting Friday. \n\n\nVisible satellite imagery this morning shows well-established \nwildfire smoke in the valleys of the Northern Rockies. The lion's\nshare of wildfire smoke is originating in Washington and Oregon,\nthough local fires are certainly contributing to ongoing poor air\nquality. Light easterly winds this morning will transport some \nsmoke out of northwest Montana today, especially just west of the\nContinental Divide. However, smoke dispersion models indicate \nsmoke will become re-established tonight as midlevel flow becomes\nmore westerly.  \n\nA ridge of high pressure will build over the Northern Rockies\nthrough the end of the week. Daytime high temperatures will\ngradually increase through Friday, when highs will be about 10\ndegrees above normal. Speaking of Friday, the upper level ridge\nwill begin to break down, resulting in increased westerly winds \nand dry conditions. These hot, dry, windy conditions will bring \nincreased fire weather concerns for Friday through the weekend.",
